WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.576
Minimal APIs make endpoint definitions
concise,

2
00:00:05.656 --> 00:00:10.852
but concise code still needs full security
design.

3
00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:18.667
A route handler can be small enough to fit
on one screen while still accepting

4
00:00:18.747 --> 00:00:25.031
external input, enforcing identity,
checking permissions,

5
00:00:25.111 --> 00:00:32.120
making state changes, returning sensitive
data, and emitting logs.

6
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:39.451
The size of the handler does not reduce
the importance of the decision it

7
00:00:39.531 --> 00:00:40.860
represents.

8
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.983
Security decisions may be easy to miss
when route handlers,

9
00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:52.139
parameter binding, validation,
authorization,

10
00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:56.448
and response shaping are close together.

11
00:00:56.528 --> 00:01:04.505
The same convenience that makes Minimal
APIs pleasant to write can make review

12
00:01:04.585 --> 00:01:09.902
harder when every endpoint chooses its own
pattern.

13
00:01:09.982 --> 00:01:18.321
A secure design makes the important
decisions visible instead of relying on local

14
00:01:18.401 --> 00:01:20.697
convention or memory.

15
00:01:20.777 --> 00:01:26.819
Minimal API security should be consistent
and reviewable.

16
00:01:26.899 --> 00:01:33.063
Reviewers should be able to tell which
endpoints are public,

17
00:01:33.143 --> 00:01:38.943
which require authentication, which
require policies,

18
00:01:39.023 --> 00:01:47.362
which inputs are accepted, which
validators run, which DTOs define the contract,

19
00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:53.484
and which response models leave the
application boundary.

20
00:01:53.564 --> 00:01:58.036
If those answers are scattered or
implicit,

21
00:01:58.116 --> 00:02:02.467
the API becomes harder to maintain safely.

22
00:02:02.547 --> 00:02:10.039
A secure Minimal API baseline includes
authentication, authorization,

23
00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:16.162
safe input boundaries, validation,
response discipline,

24
00:02:16.242 --> 00:02:24.943
request controls, browser-facing controls
where relevant, documentation review,

25
00:02:25.023 --> 00:02:27.923
and production monitoring.

26
00:02:28.003 --> 00:02:36.705
These are normal engineering concerns, not
ceremony added after the endpoint works.

27
00:02:36.785 --> 00:02:43.552
The practical mindset is to keep Minimal
APIs minimal in ceremony,

28
00:02:43.632 --> 00:02:46.170
not minimal in security.

29
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:54.951
Route handlers can remain concise while
the security model is explicit through route

30
00:02:55.031 --> 00:03:02.403
groups, metadata, named policies, DTOs,
filters, consistent results,

31
00:03:02.483 --> 00:03:04.900
and deployment review.
